  "full_response": "Data Classification Guide\n\n\nUnderstanding data classification is integral to ensuring proper data security and complying with regulations. Here are the key distinctions among the different data classification levels with examples:\n\n\n1. PUBLIC:\n\n   -Definition:_ Information that can be freely shared with anyone, without the need for special access privileges.\n\n   - _Example:_ Business's public Twitter handle accounts for sharing company updates and engaging with customers.\n\n\n2. INTERNAL_ONLY:\n\n   - _Definition:_ Content that is restricted to the company's employees. Only individuals who need to know to perform their jobs can access this information.\n\n   - _Example:_ Internal staff email threads discussing hiring or promotion policies.\n\n\n3. CONFIDENTIAL:\n\n   - _Definition:_ Information that requires restricted access and confidentiality as unauthorized disclosure could result in harm to the company or individuals' privacy.\n\n   - _Example:_ Employee contacts listed in the HR or direct contact database, as it involves sensitive personal information.\n\n\n4. CLASSIFIED_DATA:\n\n   - _Definition:_ Top-secret information that requires the highest level of protection due to its potential impact on national security, corporate interests, or privacy.\n\n   - _Example:_ Military communications, patented blueprints, customer credit card numbers, and passwords.\n\n\nIn conclusion, understanding and implementing proper data classification levels protects your company's sensitive data while complying with industry regulations and laws. Through such measures, you minimize the risk of data breaches and associated legal penalties.",
